WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Rain on a window.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.200
The two nearest drops fuse first.

3
00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.350
Then the next nearest pair. Merged, never split again.

4
00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.810
Five friends splitting into two cars can do the same.

5
00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:26.832
At first everyone is a group of one: 18 in friendships split.

6
00:00:26.832 --> 00:00:31.632
Merge the two best-matched groups, until two remain.

7
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.022
Two pairs get along best: Ada–Ben and Dev–Eva, both 5.

8
00:00:44.022 --> 00:00:50.022
A tie goes by name: A comes before D, so Ada and Ben go first.

9
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:58.424
Ada and Ben are one group now. Cai's two lines add up: 1 + 4 = 5.

10
00:00:58.424 --> 00:01:04.113
Another tie with Dev–Eva; by name, Cai joins them.

11
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.102
Last, Dev and Eva share a car. Two groups left: stop.

12
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.244
Ever seen a folder like this?

13
00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:18.822
10 sorts before 2.

14
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.738
As numbers, 2 comes first; as text, 10 does.

15
00:01:24.738 --> 00:01:28.649
Text compares one character at a time.

16
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.067
The original page compared its candidates the same way: as text.

17
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:40.200
“-1” sorts before “-5”: affinity 1 looked like the best pair.

18
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.889
The split friendship went from 3 to 9.

19
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.400
The fix: compare field by field, numbers as numbers.

20
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.978
This course also tests 10 against 2 and 2.5 against 2, so it is not right by luck.

21
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:01.140
At the hotel, every room has two beds.

22
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.496
Whichever room Cai joins, it holds three.

23
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.000
So it stops at three: no squeezing, no pretending it made two.

24
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.760
In chip design, the same merging first coarsens thousands of cells into a few clusters.

25
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:27.500
All the way, each step only looked at what was in front of it.

26
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:34.500
Pick the best pair, merge, never look back.

27
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.500
This time, the result happened to be the best.

28
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:48.500
What if an early step is wrong? See the next module.

29
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:56.600
In the lab below, change K or the capacity and watch the path change.
